# Codex provider-session access probe

This is a one-shot, bounded feasibility probe for the Context Magnet ETL. It begins with bodyless BB thread metadata, accepts `providerThreadId` only after that metadata proves `providerId: codex`, and records the server-filtered event-lane high-watermark plus a terminal `providerCheckpointId` only when that terminal event emits one.

The preferred target is `thr_c9xdr46tjs`; the runner rejects itself as target. It fetches bodyless thread metadata from the public BB REST endpoint, then only the server-filtered `thread/identity` and `turn/completed` event lane. It rejects every extra field or event type before using the identity. The live route must already own that provider thread through exactly one enabled subscription-router account, found only through `bun:sqlite` with `readonly:true, create:false` against the existing state database. It rejects a migration claim, ambiguity, draining/disabled account, or changed route revision. It never opens the router store or invokes router status.

The SQLite projection binds one enabled owner, exact provider thread, route revision, attested realCodex executable, and account home without emitting the sensitive values. The runner launches the direct executable against that home, sends `initialize`, waits for its response, sends `initialized`, then requests `thread/read(includeTurns:true)` and rejects server-originated requests. A changed revision, ambiguous owner, wrong provider, unknown request, unsupported native shape, timeout, or any limit breach must fail closed.

The direct response is sensitive. stdout before newline buffering is byte-capped, and stderr and a single line are capped independently. The probe allows one child process group at a time, has wall-time, turn, item, and fragment ceilings, and always signals then reaps its process group. It decodes only the vendored 0.153.2 receipt shapes and emits only identity metadata: BB thread, Codex `Thread.id`, Codex `Thread.sessionId`, turn/item identifiers, fragment ordinal, hook-run identifier, byte length, and SHA-256 digest. `Thread.id` and `sessionId` are independently bound and never equated.

The fixture covers the successful routed shape and native, absent, archived, disabled, divergent, unauthorized, forked, compacted, interrupted, failed, subagent, zero-work, unknown-request, every bound breach, timeout, teardown, revision-race, and ambiguous-owner failures. A complete authoritative read with zero `hookPrompt` items is reported as an explicit receipt gap, never inferred as model use; a transport or mutation failure remains unavailable. No fragment text, messages, reasoning, commands, tool data, credentials, account-home value, raw response, rollout path, or crash body is emitted or persisted.

Before and after a successful direct read, the runner compares thread `updatedAt`, status, the allowlisted-lane high-watermark and checkpoint, route revision/ownership/selection digest, a rollout identity-and-digest receipt, native-payload process identity, and the workspace dirty-state identity. The rollout is checked only inside the read-only SQLite helper: it must be a bounded regular file underneath the selected account home, stable across pre/post stat and hashing, and its path and bytes never leave that helper. The process snapshot reads `/proc` directly and only includes the fixture-attested native executable, avoiding transient probe PIDs. It re-attests every launcher-chain hop and the final native payload against the vendored schema receipt. The parent recovery envelope remains the authority for broader workspace/runtime restoration; any observed probe-fence difference is mutation evidence, not success.
